        "en" to GemDetails(
            "Prehnite is a hydrated calcium aluminium silicate. It forms at low temperature, roughly between 200 and 400 °C, mainly in cavities and fissures of basalts and other volcanic rocks altered by hot waters, along with zeolites, calcite and sometimes native copper; it also occurs in low-grade metamorphic rocks. It crystallises as botryoidal crusts and pale green to yellowish-green masses. It was the first mineral named after a person, the Dutch colonel Hendrik von Prehn, who brought it back from South Africa in the 18th century.",
            "Orthorhombic system. Rarely as tabular crystals; mostly as botryoidal, globular and fan-shaped masses. Good basal cleavage; uneven fracture. Hardness 6 to 6.5; specific gravity 2.80 to 2.95; vitreous to pearly lustre; refractive index 1.611 to 1.669; birefringence 0.021 to 0.039 (biaxial positive). Translucent, a milky apple green, sometimes with highly prized included black needles of epidote. Cut as cabochons, beads and sometimes faceted. It is attacked by acids. Its colour may fade after very long sun exposure.",
